diagnosis_text:
OSPF and forwarding on the alleged “down” core router (router16) are healthy. On router16, FRR is running with OSPF enabled for 10.2.2.0/24 and 172.30.1.0/24. The device has two active adjacencies: Full/Backup to the campus edge at 10.2.2.2 (router ID 172.16.30.254) over bond0, and Full/DR to the upstream core at 172.30.1.2 (router7) over eth2. Router16’s routing table contains OSPF-learned routes for Campus 1 networks 172.16.10/20/30.0/24 via 10.2.2.2, and router7 reciprocally learns those same prefixes via 172.30.1.1 (router16). 

Mechanism-wise, with both OSPF adjacencies in Full state, router16 is exchanging LSAs and forwarding between Campus 1 and the rest of the network. Topology-wide pings between hosts in 172.16.10/20/30 and other subnets succeed, proving end-to-end reachability across router16.

Localization: router16 bond0 (10.2.2.1) to campus edge 10.2.2.2, and router16 eth2 (172.30.1.1) to router7 eth4 (172.30.1.2) are up and routing.

Root cause: No current fault on router16; the helpdesk report appears to be a false alarm or reflected a brief prior OSPF reconvergence (neighbor uptimes ~1 minute at time of check).

Corrective fix: No recovery action required. Close the ticket as “no fault found.” Optionally review router16/neighbor logs for any recent OSPF flaps and monitor bond0 link stability to preempt future transient alarms.
